<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huiguan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A huiguan was a Chinese association, or its hall, of sojourners or migrants from the same native place. The first were founded by officials in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in the Yongle era (1403–1424); under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> they were common across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and overseas, with at least 532 in Beijing and 2,000 in 300 other cities around 1900. Most were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> of merchants or artisans, while most in Beijing lodged candidates for the imperial examinations. Huiguan kept temples, cemeteries and opera stages, held banquets, kept order among their members and represented them to the government, some acting almost as governments. In China they were dissolved in the 1950s; some overseas remain cultural organisations.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Native place was a central part of personal identity in Chinese culture. Men who moved away generally meant to return to the home of their extended family and ancestors for marriage, mourning, retirement and burial, and shared origin could bind migrants together for several generations. The first huiguan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> were probably a response to the move of the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>. Temples to regional deities, government lodgings called di or huitongguan, and trade associations resembled them, but a direct link is uncertain. The first known huiguan outside Beijing date from the Jiajing era (1522–1567).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">Guilds</a> spread and became more structured from the mid-1700s. They were called huiguan or gongsuo, the latter based on common occupation, and sought to control their trade, restrain competition and enforce agreements. After the mid-1800s they had to collect tariffs and provide firefighting, policing and other public works, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> and Hankou they became unofficial municipal governments. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> the halls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scholar-officials">scholar-officials</a> clustered in the Xuannan area near Xuanwu Gate. Kang Youwei and other activists of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days' Reform</a> organised in them, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> was founded in Beijing's Huguang Huiguan.</p><p>Overseas huiguan usually took in all migrants from an area and took on more of a governing role. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> the Cantonese district associations joined in 1862 as the Chinese Consolidated Benevolent Association, known as the Chinese Six Companies. During the anti-Chinese agitation of the 1870s newspapers accused them of importing coolies, yet they won many legal challenges to discriminatory laws. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> such communities became official administrative units in 1807 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-long">Gia Long</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Huiguan were mainly led and paid for by prominent officials or merchants. A director might inherit the post, hold it in annual rotation or be chosen by vote or lot, while hired watchmen or managers ran daily affairs. A small hall offered lodging, an altar and a meeting room; large compounds had kitchens, banquet halls, gardens and opera stages, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> at least 1,400 huiguan held regular performances. The annual meeting on the patron deity's birthday brought a feast and an opera. Many also kept coffin repositories and charitable cemeteries, and the Tung Wah Coffin Home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> received an estimated 100,000 remains sent home in the early 1900s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Huiguan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Officials needed merchants’ money for roads, schools, bridges, pagodas and book-making; the court granted merchants licences to trade in salt in return for grain shipped to the northern garrisons; and merchants pooled their capital in associations (<b>huiguan</b>, gongsuo) and partnerships and invested their profits in land.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ming-china">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</a></li><li><blockquote>Merchant guilds multiplied in the growing cities, and guild halls (<b>huiguan</b>) housed travelling traders.</blockquote><a href="/societies/qing-china">Social Hierarchy of Qing China</a></li></ul></section>
